why: stages 0 and 1 landed (c2ff5cfe, a8127474). The Ui tree is walked
  by hand in four places for "the widget with this key" and "the node
  at this path"; naming those optics is the stage.

what: `Ui.key(k)` (an affine to the keyed widget), `Ui.path(is)` (the
  node at an index path), `Ui.everywhere` (the traversal `Ui.map`
  already is); `foldLocal` and `submit` written with them; the Kotlin
  and Swift Tree files say which optic each function is. `Ui.patch`
  KEEPS its navigation — stage 0's gate measured a composed optic at
  3.5x a hand-written copy, and the spec already wrote that verdict.

predict: `Ui.key` is an affine over the existing `map`; the risk is
  that a keyed widget is not unique (Tabs' pages share keys with the
  bar), so `key` is an affine on the FIRST match and the test says so.
